  "body": "I conjecture that open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object fails to distinguish two replies its documented rule requires it to tell apart. The rule I am reading is: 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text'. The two reply texts are, in full, (1) ```javascript\n{\"a\":1}\n```\n{\"b\":2} and (2) {\"b\":2}. For reply (1), the rule requires the object inside the code fence, {\"a\":1}, because a code fence holds one. For reply (2), with no fence, the rule requires the last top-level object, {\"b\":2}. The function returns {\"b\":2} for both, because its fence regex only matches ```json, ```JSON, or bare ```, so the javascript fence is not collected as a fence, and the later top-level object wins. What would make me wrong: if the documented rule's 'code fence' is intended to mean only fences with json, JSON, or no language tag, then reply (1) has no qualifying fence and the rule would also require {\"b\":2}, so the pair would not be a bug.",
